# Target C: exact-contact replacement with a contact budget

Date: 2026-10-05. Base main: `ad8809fd96c2c0af9a6de4d965e9ab33efe092c6`.
State: `2026-10-05.audit.59`.
Classification: **proved candidate synthesis awaiting independent audit**;
artifact lifecycle **unreviewed**. This is a bookkeeping endpoint, not a
packing-existence theorem or a claim of a novel normalization principle.
Level I and C-II remain open.

## 1. Exact chain

```
actual finite exact-contact packet with no contact-count increase
+ destruction of at least one old diameter and no replacement diameter
-> same-cardinality triangle-free competitor with smaller lexicographic
   (diameter, contact count, diameter-pair count)
-> contradict canonical normalization.
```

Unlike a strict sub-D exchange, this endpoint need not destroy every old
active diameter. It is therefore usable in both exhaustive C-II branches,
independently of s_Z, when actual finite packet geometry is supplied.
Audit.59 excludes a mean-area guarantee for a strict packet; it does not
exclude an explicitly certified exact-contact packet.

## 2. Finite replacement theorem

Let A be a triangle-free global diameter minimizer, contact-minimal among
triangle-free global minimizers and then diameter-pair-minimal among those
with the minimum contact count. Write C,H for its actual unit-contact and
diameter-pair graphs and D for its diameter.

Choose R subset A and an actual finite set Y with |Y|=|R|. Let

    t=number of C edges incident to R,
    d=number of H edges incident to R.

Assume:

1. every pair in Y and every pair from Y to A minus R has distance >=1;
2. every such distance is strictly below D;
3. the new source A'=(A minus R) union Y is triangle-free;
4. j, the number of new unit contacts with at least one endpoint in Y,
   satisfies j<=t;
5. either j<t, or d>0.

Then such a packet cannot exist for A.

**Proof.** All new points are distinct and distinct from retained points
by item 1, so cardinality is unchanged. The new source is unit-separated,
triangle-free and has diameter at most D. If its diameter is below D,
global minimality fails. Otherwise it is a triangle-free global minimizer.
Its exact contact count is

    |C(A')|=|C|-t+j.

If j<t, contact minimality fails. If j=t, every new-involving pair is
strictly below D by item 2, so its exact diameter-pair count is

    |H(A')|=|H|-d.

Since d>0, secondary minimality fails. QED.

The strict upper condition can be checked directly. It also follows if
all Y sites lie in int(conv A): an interior point cannot be a diameter
endpoint of that compact convex hull. No isolate is dropped; its label
is retained or explicitly replaced.

All contacts between inserted and retained points count in j. Counting
only the packet's internal contacts is insufficient. Likewise a forest
inside Y does not prove item 3 if cross contacts are present: triangles
using one or two inserted points must also be excluded.

## 3. Why two-contact packets cannot supply a new mechanism

If every inserted site is strictly clear of retained points, a sufficiently
small common dilation of Y opens all its internal unit contacts. All
inactive lower and strict upper inequalities persist by their finite
margins. Thus internal exact contacts alone reduce to strict refill;
a genuine exact-contact route needs retained-source anchor contacts.

More generally any finite feasible packet with at most two new unit
contacts, strict upper bounds and unchanged retained points can be
perturbed to open all its new contacts.

**Proof.** Let the gradients of these at most two squared-distance
constraints be rows on the inserted coordinates. Unless a positive
combination of these rows is zero, elementary separation gives a direction
strictly increasing both active lower distances. A small straight step
opens them and preserves every inactive inequality.

One row cannot vanish, since it contains a nonzero unit-edge vector at
an inserted endpoint. Two rows can be negative proportional only when
they are two cross edges joining the same inserted point to two opposite
retained unit neighbors. Distinct internal edges cannot have that
property: their nonzero coordinate supports differ, except if they were
the same unordered edge, which is counted only once. An internal and a
cross edge likewise have different supports. Two cross edges at different
inserted points have disjoint supports.

In the exceptional case, move that inserted point perpendicularly to
the line of its two retained neighbors. Both squared unit distances
become 1+epsilon^2. Other inserted points stay fixed, and all remaining
pairs have finite strict margins. This again opens every new contact.
QED.

Consequently if R removes an old contact, an equal-cardinality interior
packet has at least three new unit contacts. Otherwise the preceding
perturbation would give an equal-cardinality strict refill, contradicting
contact minimality. This excludes every putative two-contact-budget
packet for one loaded degree-two vertex plus any number of isolates.
An internal unit dimer or two-edge path is therefore not a distinct
closure route, even if initially written with exact unit lengths.

The same first-order separation argument gives a necessary pinned
nonnegative equilibrium stress for every feasible equal-cardinality
packet replacing a contact-active deletion: without one, a contact-opening
direction produces the forbidden strict refill. This is only necessary;
the opposite-anchor example above shows why first-order stress alone
does not certify a finite obstruction. No kernel or rank claim is imported
from the original core to the new packet.

For a single feasible replacement site x in int K, audit.59 gives a stronger
geometric statement. Its retained unit neighbors must strictly surround
x: otherwise there is a nonzero direction w with

    (x-a) dot w >=0

for every unit neighbor a. The step x+epsilon*w opens every such unit
distance, including those whose linear derivative is zero, because the
quadratic term is positive. All other inequalities remain strict. Thus
there must be at least three unit neighbors and zero must lie in the
interior of the convex hull of their unit vectors. This is a local pinned
site statement, not positive equilibrium on original isolate coordinates.

A first genuinely anchored candidate uses a loaded degree-three deletion
and an interior site at unit distance from three retained vertices,
strictly farther than one from every other retained point. The three
neighbors must strictly surround the site, and their mutual unit edges
must be absent to keep the new source triangle-free. With exactly three
new contacts, the contact budget is unchanged and an old diameter is
removed. Section 2 then contradicts normalization. Existence of that
three-anchor site is unproved; an arbitrary unit circumcenter does not
satisfy clearance or triangle-freeness automatically.

A fan reflection preserves its two old contacts. Subsequent replacement
of only contact isolates has zero remaining contact budget. A wall-touching
jammed site that adds contacts is not an equal-budget repair. Additional
old contacts must be removed, or a strict sub-D source must be proved
independently of contact counting.

## 4. Composition and limits

This theorem supplies the spendable quantity for audit.59's explicit
exact-contact route: removed actual contacts, together with one removed
actual diameter. It reduces the original-source packet target from
paying a diameter cover to a finite contact-budget comparison. A packet
creating a triangle cannot use the contact/diameter normalization endpoint;
its diameter must be proved strictly smaller directly.

No audit.52--59 theorem presently cited in this argument produces the
required anchored three-contact site or general packet with certified retained-source
clearance. Positive residual, a large wall, a near-unit blocker matching,
and old strict isolate margins do not certify them. The fan octagon
capacity-loss regression also prevents transporting those margins through
the reflection. Both full-core and macroscopic-container existence
obligations remain open; no authoritative lifecycle transition is made.

## 5. Validation

The proof is finite analytic contact and diameter counting. No numerical
verifier is needed or claimed. Repository checks validate metadata and
state consistency, not packet existence or Level I closure.
